ximately equal.\n4. Linearity: The relationship between factors and the response should be linear.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nFactorial designs are particularly useful when:\n- Multiple factors are believed to influence a response variable.\n- Interaction effects between factors are of interest.\n- The researcher aims to optimize a process or product.\nSelection criteria include the number of factors, the levels of each factor, and the resources available for experimentation.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of factorial designs typically involves:\n1. Conducting an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine the significance of main effects and interactions.\n2. Estimating the effects using regression analysis.\n3. Performing post-hoc tests if necessary to explore differences between factor levels.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Efficiently evaluates multiple factors and their interactions.\n- Provides comprehensive insights into the effects of factors on the response variable.\n- Can lead to better optimization of processes due to the understanding of interactions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Requires a larger number of experimental runs, especially in full factorial designs, which can be resource-intensive.\n- Complexity increases with the number of factors and levels, making analysis and interpretation more challenging.\n- Assumptions must be met for valid conclusions, which may not always be the case in practical scenarios.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include fractional factorial designs, response surface methodology, and Taguchi methods. These alternatives may be considered when resources are limited or when the number of factors is very high.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing a factorial design, researchers should consider:\n- The selection of appropriate factor levels based on prior knowledge or pilot studies.\n- Randomization of exp